   "steps": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "With $N = 13$ the dashed lines enclose the blue bars $u_5$ to $u_{13}$. That is $9$ terms, and from part (b) their sum is $\\frac{9}{2}(34 + 2) = 162$. Adding $\\sum_{r=14}^{N} u_r = -162$ means the whole sum from $u_5$ to $u_N$ must be $162 + (-162) = 0$.",
     "set": {
      "N": 13
     },
     "show": [
      "sumLabel"
     ],
     "hide": [
      "pairLabel"
     ],
     "highlight": [
      "startLine",
      "endLine"
     ]
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "Drag $N$ to the right. Each new bar is negative (for example $u_{14} = -2$ and $u_{15} = -6$), so the running sum falls. At $N = 18$ the sum is still positive: the negative bars are not yet big enough to cancel the positive ones.",
     "set": {
      "N": 18
     }
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "At $N = 22$ the sum reads $0$. The bars cancel in pairs: $34$ with $-34$, $30$ with $-30$, and so on down to $2$ with $-2$. The algebra gives the same result: $\\frac{m}{2}[2(34) + (m-1)(-4)] = \\frac{m}{2}(72 - 4m) = 0$, so $m = 18$ (reject $m = 0$). Writing this sum is the first M1, and $m = 18$ is the A1ft. Keep the $2a$ inside the bracket: with just $a$ the equation comes out wrong.",
     "set": {
      "N": 22
     },
     "show": [
      "pairLabel"
     ],
     "highlight": [
      "b5",
      "b22"
     ]
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "Count the coloured bars from $u_5$: there are $18$ of them, and the last one is $u_{22}$. So $N = 5 + 18 - 1 = 22$, not $5 + 18 = 23$. This is the final M1. Drag $N$ to $23$ and the sum becomes $-38$, not $0$.",
     "set": {
      "N": 22
     },
     "highlight": [
      "endMark",
      "endLine"
     ]
    }
